 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "Adiabats obey a law",
   "text": "For an adiabatic change of an ideal gas, \\(PV^{\\gamma}=\\text{const}\\). With \\(P=nRT/V\\) this becomes \\(TV^{\\gamma-1}=\\text{const}\\). \\(\\gamma\\) is **Beyond the book** (Physics F8): your book never defines it, and the final result below does not depend on its value.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "ph": 4
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 6500
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Apply it to b → c",
   "text": "Point \\(b\\) has \\((T_1, V_2)\\) and point \\(c\\) has \\((T_2, V_3)\\). Both lie on the same adiabat, so \\(T_1V_2^{\\gamma-1}=T_2V_3^{\\gamma-1}\\).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "ph": 4
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 5200
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Apply it to d → a",
   "text": "Point \\(d\\) has \\((T_2, V_4)\\) and point \\(a\\) has \\((T_1, V_1)\\) on the other adiabat: \\(T_1V_1^{\\gamma-1}=T_2V_4^{\\gamma-1}\\).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "ph": 4
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 5200
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "Divide the two equations",
   "text": "Divide the first equation by the second. \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\) cancel completely and what is left is \\(\\left(\\dfrac{V_2}{V_1}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}=\\left(\\dfrac{V_3}{V_4}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}\\).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "ph": 4
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 6000
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "Take the root",
   "text": "Both sides have the same power \\(\\gamma-1\\), so the bases are equal: \\(\\dfrac{V_2}{V_1}=\\dfrac{V_3}{V_4}\\). On the \\(\\ln V\\) ruler the **orange** and **blue** brackets have the same length.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "ph": 4
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 6000
  },
  {
   "id": "s5",
   "title": "A picture on log axes",
   "text": "On log–log axes an isotherm is a line of slope \\(-1\\) and an adiabat a line of slope \\(-\\gamma\\). The Carnot loop becomes a **parallelogram**, and opposite sides of a parallelogram have equal horizontal extent. That is the same result seen as a picture. Try \\(\\gamma\\) and the temperatures.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "ph": 4,
      "lg": 1
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ],
   "dwell": 6500
  }
 ],
